<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Document Conversion</title>
<style>
  @page { size: A4; margin: 0; }
  .page {
    width: 794px; height: 1123px;
    padding: 50px 60px; box-sizing: border-box;
    overflow: hidden; page-break-after: always;
    position: relative;
    font-family: "DejaVu Serif", "Liberation Serif", Georgia, "Helvetica", Arial, sans-serif;
  }
  .page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }
  h1, h2, h3, h4 { font-weight: bold; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  td, th { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }
  .two-column { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; }
  .two-column > div { width: 49%; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0;">10 SALGADO AND TÁURIZ</h2>
    <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">
      <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top: 20px; font-size: 10pt;">Individuals only partially order the items, rather than ordering them completely; (2) scales have different numbers of items; (3) not all alternatives ranked by respondents are scored; (4) scales are not equivalent for different individuals with different characteristics or involve different normative trans-formations on the basis of respondent characteris-tics; (5) scored alternatives are differentially weighted; and (6) the inventory has normative data that is not quasi-invasive.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 10pt;">Our database are the Gordon Personal Profile Inventory (Gordon, 1933), the PIP-MFC (Higgsdale et al., 2006), the Self-Description Inven-tory (Higgsdale, 1954), the ESQ (Jackson, 2002), and the Assessment Individual Motivation (AIM, 1994).</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 10pt;">Finally, we classified inventories as normative if it yielded scores that possess the empirical properties of absolute measures. This is the case of the inventories in which items representing different degrees of a trait have been never paired with a normative data.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 10pt;">The MBTI and the "Need of Achievement" question-naire (Finneman, 1975) are representative examples of normative FC questionnaires. Following the stan-dards of the American Psychological Association for the development of research reports (APA, 2010), and Communications Board Workgroup (2008), we conducted the regression and subset Sackett (2012) on data reporting in meta-analysis, we have included an appendix with the totality of studies and validity coefficients that have been used in our meta-analysis (see Appendix B).</p>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="two-column">
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Artifact distributions</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Predictor reliability. The reliability of the personality dimensions was estimated from the coefficients reported in the studies included in our meta-analysis. We used meta-analyses to infer internal consistency coefficients as estimates of reliability. We developed an empirical distribution of Cronbach's coefficients for the Big Five. The predictor reliability estimates were used to eliminate artifactual errors, and to estimate the deviation of p and q from the theoretical value of the validity of the Big Five personality dimensions. For each personality dimension, a reliability distribution was estimated.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The average reliability was <span data-field="average_reliability">{{ average_reliability }}</span>, and the standard deviation of the reliability was <span data-field="std_dev_reliability">{{ std_dev_reliability }}</span>. We also examined whether the three types of FC scoring systems showed different levels of reliability, but they proved to be very similar. Therefore, we used the average reliability in the meta-analysis calculations. Table 1 presents a summary of artifact distributions. In Table 1, we have also included the reliabilities found by Jones and Viswanesan (1999) in their meta-analysis of Big Five reliability. As can be seen, our estimates are very similar to the estimates found by Jones and Viswanesan and are also similar to the estimates used in previous meta-analyses (e.g., Barick & Mount, 1991; Hurtz & Donovan, 2000; Salgado, 1997, 2003).</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Although we used internal consistency coeffi-cients, this decision may be controversial for some researchers. For example, Higgsdale et al. (2006) point out that Cronbach's alpha is not an appropriate coefficient for estimating the reliability of FC inventories because the responses to the items are not independent, and therefore, observed item covariances are not accurate estimates of the true item covariances. They suggested that test-retest coefficients are the appropriate estimates. For this reason, we also developed an empirical distribution of test-retest coefficients for the Big Five, which is presented in the studies included in our database. The average test-retest reliability was <span data-field="average_test_retest_reliability">{{ average_test_retest_reliability }}</span>, and the standard deviation of the test-retest reliability was <span data-field="std_dev_test_retest_reliability">{{ std_dev_test_retest_reliability }}</span>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Artifact distributions</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Range restriction distributions. The distributions for range restriction were based on the following three strategies: (1) Some range restriction values were obtained from the studies that reported both restricted and unrestricted stan-dard deviations; (2) Another group of range restriction values were obtained using the population values reported in the manual of the various inventories, and (3) a third group of range restriction values was obtained using the reported selection ratios.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The range restriction values were obtained using the formula derived by Schmidt, Hunter, and Wolfe (1991). This triple strategy produced a large number of range restriction estimates, and we grouped them according to the personality dimensions. The average range restriction was <span data-field="average_range_restriction">{{ average_range_restriction }}</span> for emotional stability, conscientiousness, extraversion, openness, and agreeableness, and <span data-field="average_range_restriction_other">{{ average_range_restriction_other }}</span> for the remaining dimensions.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">These values were used when no more specific ones were available. We also found <span data-field="number_of_values">{{ number_of_values }}</span> values of range restriction for the Big Five.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0;">12 SALGADO AND TÁURIZ</h2>
    <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">
      <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top: 20px; font-size: 10pt;">with SS inventories. This is an important finding as some researchers had suggested that FC inventories would show a different level of reliability than SS questionnaires.</p>

  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Criterion reliability.</h3>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">In this research, six types of criteria were used: (1) supervisory ratings of job performance, (2) productivity data (e.g., sales), (3) training proficiency, (4) grade point average, (5) occupational performance, and (6) academic performance. This choice was made for two reasons: (1) Previous meta-analyses of personality measures used some of these types of criteria, and one of our objectives was to provide a comparison with those meta-analyses. (2) We wanted to retain the same criteria, or others were used in a very small number of studies, or were totally absent from our database; therefore, we would not be able to carry out meta-analyses in these cases.</p>

  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Meta-analytic methods.</h3>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">In this section, we report information regarding the criterion reliability and, consequently, the empirical distributions for the six criteria. For the first criterion, a number of studies provided reliability coefficients for estimating criterion reliability. For job performance ratings, the coefficient of interest is inter-rater reliability. For the other criteria, the coefficient of interest is omitted (Hunter, 1986; Sackett, 2003). This is because this type of criterion is used in the correction for attenuation, which is the most common use of the criterion in meta-analysis. We used the meta-analytic method of Hunter and Schmidt (1998) to estimate the criterion reliability. The artifactual errors are estimated using the population values reported in the manual of the various inventories, and (3) a third group of range restriction values was obtained using the reported selection ratios.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The average coefficient was <span data-field="average_criterion_reliability">{{ average_criterion_reliability }}</span>. The average coefficient for job performance ratings was <span data-field="avg_job_perf_rel">{{ avg_job_perf_rel }}</span>, and for productivity data was <span data-field="avg_prod_data_rel">{{ avg_prod_data_rel }}</span>. For training proficiency, the average coefficient was <span data-field="avg_train_prof_rel">{{ avg_train_prof_rel }}</span>, for grade point average, <span data-field="avg_gpa_rel">{{ avg_gpa_rel }}</span>, for occupational performance, <span data-field="avg_occ_perf_rel">{{ avg_occ_perf_rel }}</span>, and for academic performance, <span data-field="avg_acad_perf_rel">{{ avg_acad_perf_rel }}</span>.</p>

  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Table 2</h3>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Distributions of criteria reliability</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Criterion</th>
        <th>Mean</th>
        <th>SD</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Job performance ratings</td>
        <td>.52</td>
        <td>.05</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Training proficiency</td>
        <td>.50</td>
        <td>.05</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Productivity data</td>
        <td>.83</td>
        <td>.07</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Grade point average</td>
        <td>.80</td>
        <td>.10</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Occupational performance</td>
        <td>.61</td>
        <td>.13</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Academic performance</td>
        <td>.81</td>
        <td>.09</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0;">13</h2>
    <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">
      <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top: 20px; font-size: 10pt;">We used the psychometric meta-analysis methods developed by Hunter and Schmidt (1998), and implemented in a software program by Schmidt and Wolfe (1991). This software includes some recent advances in indirect range restriction (IRR). According to Hunter, Schmidt, and Le (2006), IRR is the most common case of range restriction in meta-analysis. It is present in all concurrent validity studies and in practically all predictive validity studies.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 10pt;">In a series of studies, Schmidt and his colleagues have demonstrated the effects of the IRR correction on the validity of general mental ability (GMA), on the validity of specific cognitive abilities, and on the validity of personality traits. They have also shown that the use of IRR can improve the precision of the estimates of the validity of personality traits.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 10pt;">The meta-analytic methods used in this study are based on the methods of Hunter and Schmidt (1998). The methods are used to correct for attenuation, which is the attenuation of the true correlation between the predictor and the criterion. The artifactual errors are estimated using the population values reported in the manual of the various inventories, and (3) a third group of range restriction values was obtained using the reported selection ratios.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 10pt;">The average coefficient was <span data-field="average_criterion_reliability_2">{{ average_criterion_reliability_2 }}</span>. The average coefficient for job performance ratings was <span data-field="avg_job_perf_rel_2">{{ avg_job_perf_rel_2 }}</span>, and for productivity data was <span data-field="avg_prod_data_rel_2">{{ avg_prod_data_rel_2 }}</span>. For training proficiency, the average coefficient was <span data-field="avg_train_prof_rel_2">{{ avg_train_prof_rel_2 }}</span>, for grade point average, <span data-field="avg_gpa_rel_2">{{ avg_gpa_rel_2 }}</span>, for occupational performance, <span data-field="avg_occ_perf_rel_2">{{ avg_occ_perf_rel_2 }}</span>, and for academic performance, <span data-field="avg_acad_perf_rel_2">{{ avg_acad_perf_rel_2 }}</span>.</p>

  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Table 3</h3>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Distributions of range restriction</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Personality dimension</th>
        <th>Mean</th>
        <th>SD</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for item in personality_dimensions %}
      <tr>
        <td><span data-field="personality_dimensions[{{ loop.index0 }}].name">{{ item.name }}</span></td>
        <td><span data-field="personality_dimensions[{{ loop.index0 }}].mean">{{ item.mean }}</span></td>
        <td><span data-field="personality_dimensions[{{ loop.index0 }}].sd">{{ item.sd }}</span></td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p style="margin-top: 20px; font-size: 10pt;">The range restriction values were obtained using the formula derived by Schmidt, Hunter, and Wolfe (1991). The range restriction values were obtained using the population values reported in the manual of the various inventories, and (3) a third group of range restriction values was obtained using the reported selection ratios.</p>
