  <p>Study limitations and strengths</p>
  <p>This research also has some limitations that should be</p>
  <p>mentioned. First, some criteria were not examined</p>
  <p>(i.e., job absenteeism or turnover) because no studies</p>
  <p>were available on these criteria. Second, the number</p>
  <p>of studies is small in some cases or the total</p>
  <p>sample size is small (see Salgado, 1998). Conse-</p>
  <p>quently, the estimates reported here could change as</p>
  <p>the number of studies or the sample size is enlarged.</p>
  <p>Third, the meta-analysis is based on the FC form</p>
  <p>based on the FFM have larger validity than the</p>
  <p>normative form. Previous research found that SS inventories based on the FFM showed</p>
  <p>larger validity (Salgado, 2003; see also</p>
  <p>Barrick & Mount, 1991; Barrick et al., 2001; Hurrnd</p>
  <p>and Mount, 2000; Salgado, 1997). These two contributions</p>
  <p>that quasi-ipsative questionnaires are more valid</p>
  <p>procedures for assessing personality in academic and</p>
  <p>occupational settings. Until now, previous meta-</p>
  <p>analyses had examined this issue. It was therefore</p>
  <p>unknown that quasi-ipsative questionnaires were such</p>
  <p>excellent methods for making personnel decisions.</p>
  <p>The comparison between normative FC and</p>
  <p>ipsative inventories suggests that the latter are</p>
  <p>similarly or slightly more valid, although the number</p>
  <p>of studies is relatively small to reach a firm</p>
  <p>conclusion. Comparing the results of the previous</p>
  <p>meta-analyses, conducted with SS inventories, with</p>
  <p>those of the normative and ipsative FC</p>
  <p>inventories, it cannot be concluded that the SS</p>
  <p>questionnaires are superior in validity.</p>
  <p style="text-align: right; font-size: 10pt;">FF, FORCED-CHOICE INVENTORIES AND PERFORMANCE 21</p>
  <p>FC and SS personality measures assess different</p>
  <p>aspects or elements of the same personality construct</p>
  <p>or they essentially assess the same elements is an issue</p>
  <p>that has been debated in the literature. This meta-</p>
  <p>analysis, based on our results, we cannot make firm</p>
  <p>conclusions about this question. Previous research</p>
  <p>did not respond to it either. Therefore, this is an open</p>
  <p>question from the viewpoint of the meta-analysis</p>
  <p>practical points of view. From the theoretical it makes</p>
  <p>sense to put together the two types of measures in</p>
  <p>order to have a better assessment of personality</p>
  <p>dimensions. On the other hand, from a practical</p>
  <p>point of view, if FC and SS measures are correlated</p>
  <p>but they are relatively independent, then FC mea-</p>
  <p>sures could show incremental validity over SS</p>
  <p>measures for predicting academic and occupational</p>
  <p>performance. In our opinion, this last issue is one that</p>
  <p>deserves future research. Both types of techniques</p>
  <p>may be linked in ways that are not yet clear.</p>
  <p>In the case of FC scores, individuals would focus on comparing alter-</p>
  <p>natives to each other in order to create a ranking or</p>
  <p>hierarchy. In the case of SS scores, and according to</p>
  <p>Hogan (1992), the individual would compare himself to</p>
  <p>other people: Meglin and Ravlin (1998) suggested</p>
  <p>that the FC form is more appropriate for assessing</p>
  <p>phenomenon, and even when it is technically possible</p>
  <p>to use SS scores to ipsative ones and to recover</p>
  <p>the original scores, the meta-analysis does not give</p>
  <p>meaning that was modeled by each kind of measure.</p>
  <p>Furthermore, as Brown and Maydew-Olivares (2011)</p>
  <p>have suggested, FC forms are more cognitively</p>
  <p>demanding than SS forms. In the case of FC,</p>
  <p>the forms, the block size is positively related with the</p>
  <p>cognitive load of the respondent because there are rit-</p>
  <p>uals of comparison. Consequently, comparing</p>
  <p>FC and SS responses to the same items might</p>
  <p>produce erroneous conclusions because the validity</p>
  <p>of the two forms is not the same.</p>
  <p>In summary, this meta-analysis comprehensively</p>
  <p>reviewed the validity of the Big Five personality</p>
  <p>predictors of academic and occupational performance</p>
  <p>(e.g., GPA) and</p>
  <p>occupational performance (e.g., job performance</p>
  <p>ratings, training proficiency, productivity). The re-</p>
  <p>sults showed that the quasi-ipsative FC measures of</p>
